  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/nss.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "National Service Scheme is a student – centered programme and it is complementary to education. It is a noble experiment in academic extension. It inculcates the spirit of voluntary work among students and teachers through sustained community interaction. It brings our academic institutions closer to the society. It is a link between the campus and community, the college and village, knowledge and action. The overall aim of NSS is the Personality Development of students through community service. It gives an extension dimension to Higher Education system and orients the student youth to community service.\nNational Service Scheme, NSS, was formally launched on 24th September, 1969, the birth centenary of the Father of the Nation. Starting with an enrolment of 40000 students in 37 Universities covering all states, the coverage of NSS students increased to about 26.6 lakhs (2006-07) in 198 Universities, 9117 colleges,1196 Technical Institutions, 7542 Plus Two Schools and 41 Plus Two Vocational Higher Secondary Directorates. During its 39 years of fruitful existence more than 2.7crore students have so far benefited from this largest student-volunteer ship programme in the world.\nThe N.S.S. Unit No: 120 of Govt. Engineering College, Barton Hill has always been there for the integrated development of the college as well as the society. In the present academic year the Unit has decided to contribute a lot with its regular and other activities. Many new programs have been introduced under the guidance of our N.S.S. Programme Officer Ambili N, Assistant Professor, Department of ECE."
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/ieee.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "“When technology meets talent, the outcomes are sure to be \nrevolutionary”\nIEEE Student Branch,The IEEE Student Branch Government Engineering College Barton Hill is the brainchild of an energetic and dedicated team whose boundless passion for innovation is truly remarkable. Their exceptional commitment has produced numerous accomplished professionals who have made significant contributions to the tech industry. Since its inception in 2005, the Student Branch has continually impressed, always raising the bar with each milestone achieved. In the year 2022 alone, they have been honored with the IEEE MGA Regional Exemplary\nStudent Branch Award, the IEEE R10 Outstanding Student Branch Award, and the prestigious Darrel Chong Student Activity Award in the Gold Category for their annual mega flagship event, Exordium. Adding to their list of achievements, three of their volunteers were recognized by the IEEE India Council as Outstanding IEEE Professional Volunteer, Outstanding IEEE WIE Volunteer, and Outstanding IEEE Student Volunteer. These recognitions serve as a testament to the exceptional dedication and hard work of the IEEE SB GECBH team.\nThe IEEE student branch in GECBH at present has active chapters of\nIEEE Robotics and Automation Society\nIEEE Computer Society\nIEEE Antenna and Propagation Society\nIEEE Microwave Theory and Techniques Society\nIEEE Women in Engineering Affinity Group\nIEEE Special Interest Group in Humanitarian Activities (SIGHT)\nExecutive Committee\nCounselor : Prof. Josna V R (Assistant Professor, Department of Information Technology)\nChair : Goutham Rajesh (gouthamrajesh@ieee.org | +91 90742 15043)\nSecretary : Raihana Alam P A (raihana.alam1511@ieee.org | +91 90371 42644)\nVice Chair : Devanarayanan S V (devanarayanansv@ieee.org | +91 94958 61692)\nTo know more about us, visit our official website\n[🌐 Visit IEEE GECBH](https://www.ieeegecbh.org/)"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/istesc.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "ISTE is a national professional body consisting of nearly 40,000 engineering teachers and academicians. Our College ISTE chapter is functioning with more than 400 student members. The ISTE student chapter is the most vibrant and active chapter in the campus. It organizes Talks by experts, Quiz, Paper presentation, Mock Interviews, Robotics Contest, Hands-on events etc. The club was open to students of all branches. Student Teacher Program was yet another undertaking to familiarize the students in engineering aspects that are beyond the scope of syllabus.\n[Visit ISTEG ECB](https://istegecb.in/)\nEvents Organized by ISTE GECBH Students Chapter\nThe 18th Annual ISTE State Student Convention\nStudent Teacher Programme: A weeklong coding workshop held as part of the student teacher program under Bartonoidz which saw a footfall of 30+ Students. The workshop covered portions like Datatypes control structures, file management, Arduino programming etc.\nASK- Alternative for Sharing Knowledge: ASK was held by ISTE GEC students chapter to encourage technical and general knowledge sharing among the students of our college. The prelims was conducted on April 7, 2017 and about 25 teams participated among which 5 teams were finalized and the finals of the competition was held on 13 April 2017.\nBot-TECH Workshop : BOT-TECH was organized exclusively for Bartonoidz members. The workshop was meant to be an interest development session which included dancing humanoids, Zumo robot wrestling and mini Quadcopters.\nMechatronics system workshop: A 3 Day workshop on Mechatronic Systems was conducted such that the participants get a clear idea on which all sensors and actuators are available for specific applications.\nNikola Tesla Scholarship: Nikola Tesla Scholarship is an event conducted to provide Financial aid to meritorious 1st The event was divided into Written Prelims, Group Discussion and Final Interview.\nMINI QUADCOPTER WORKSHOP: Bartonoidz conducted a two day workshop on Mini Quadcopter where the theory session dealt with theoretical aspects of flying bodies, basic aerodynamics, the components used in Quadcopters as well as balancing of drones.\nBart-Ex- Technical Expo : Bartonoidz and ISTE GEC students’ chapter organized a technical exhibition during Aagneya, the techno cultural fest of GEC Barton Hill with the projects made by the students. There were about 30 projects from the students and other projects made individually by the seniors were also exhibited.\nCodify with Mayank Bhura: A two day workshop on General Programming was organized by Mayank Bhura,software engineer at Google,Hyderabad. Topics such as introduction to basic algorithm and simplification methods,advanced coding methods, introduction to python coding and JAVA coding. A one hour interactive session was also conducted in which participants had an opportunity to clear their doubts about python and simplified algorithm building techniques.\nDigital India Quiz: A Quiz programme on “Digital India”for the Engineering College Students of Kerala was held at Government Engineering College, Barton hill, Trivandrum and attractive cash prizes and certificates were offered for the winners."
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/personal-growth-centre.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "The Personal Growth Centre at Government Engineering College, Barton Hill, is committed to fostering emotional well-being, self-awareness, and resilience among students and faculty. It serves as a safe and inclusive space that encourages open conversations around mental health and personal development. As part of our ongoing efforts to promote holistic growth, the college, in collaboration with Let’s Live, a youth-led mental health NGO based in Trivandrum, has initiated the #Unfiltered – Mental Health Campus Outreach Programme under the broader project “Cultivating Resilience.”\nCounselling Support\nTo provide continuous emotional and psychological support, the college has appointed a visiting counsellor, Ms. Sreelekshmi M. S., who offers one-on-one counselling sessions on all Wednesdays (rescheduled in case of holidays or exams). Students can request appointments through a Google form circulated in their class groups. Typically, 4–5 students are counselled each week, ensuring personal attention and confidentiality. Joined from 7/8/25 as visiting councillor on all Wednesdays (day changes in case of holidays or exam days). As per Council minutes 8.7.25 & Appointment order Counsellor no GECBH/567/2025-E2 dt .04-08- 2025. 4-5 students counselled each day as per request posted through Google form circulated in class groups.\nLooking Ahead\nThe Personal Growth Centre envisions building a mentally healthier, more resilient campus where every student feels heard, supported, and empowered. Through collaborative initiatives like #Unfiltered and ongoing counselling services, the Centre continues to cultivate empathy, awareness, and strength — one conversation at a time.\nChairperson\nDr. Shenil P S\nHoD, EEE\nCo-chairperson\nSmt. Dhanya C K\nAsst. Prof. in IT"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/csi.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "Computer Society of India established in the year 1965 is today the largest it professionals Society in India. The purposes of the Society are scientific and educational advancement of the theory and practice of computer science, computer engineering and technology, systems science and engineering, information processing and related arts and sciences. The mission of the CSI is to facilitate research, knowledge sharing and career enhancement for all categories of it professionals, while simultaneously inspiring and nurturing new entrants into the industry and helping them to integrate into the IT community. Today, the CSI has 66 chapters all over India, 381 student branches, and more than 40,000 members, including India’s most famous it industry leaders and dedicated academicians.\nCSI Student Branch GECBH is one of the active technical society of Government Engineering College Barton Hill, Trivandrum. The body is guided by the Principal along with Staff Advisor Professor Josna of department Information Technology. The main members are of the Information Technology department along with the the society providing a free opportunity for other department students to join India's largest professional association through the CSI BETA program.\nThe executive student committee headed by Kelvin Patric along with Gokul, Rahul, Shaino, Adithyan, Meenu, Malavika, Nayana, Abhishek and Risvana. Talk sessions, project guidance, workshops, competitions and many other events are conducted by the SB for the betterment of each and every student of the college who wishes to have a professional knowledge in the field of Information Technology. Events are usually conducted based on student needs. The society will always work for the betterment of students along with progress of GECBH to achieve greater heights.\nTo know more about us, visit our official website\n[💻 Visit CSI GECBH](https://csigecbh.in/)"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/iedc.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "The Innovation and Entrepreneurship Development Cell (IEDC) of Government Engineering College Barton Hill continues to provide a strong platform for fostering innovation, nurturing young entrepreneurial talent, and driving impactful student-led initiatives. The Annual General Meeting (AGM) held on 18th July 2025 marked the official transition to a new leadership team for the academic year 2025–26, reflecting on the achievements of the past year and setting the vision for the year ahead.\nCEO: Adarsh S Suresh |8590348840\nCTO: Shiyas S | 85930 31321\nCOO: Rohan R | S 81293 82162\nCOO: Muralee Krishnan Kartha P. U. | 9747644646\nCCoO: Airine Johns | 85904 31759\nCFO: S P Bhagath | 85477 13946\nCCO: Asna Azad | 89218 062876\nCMO: Gautham S | 80895 54818\nCEO: Niranjan Krishna S\nCTO: Joshith S Jacob\nCOO: Ramharan A V\nCCoO: Nieraj S Saji\nCFO: Devu S S\nCCO: Anagha Suresh\nCMO: Madhav P Nair\nIPR LEAD: Ajmal Mohammad Azim\nWomen Entrepreneurship Lead: Keerthana Suresh\nProf. Jayasree P(90480 67190), Dr. Suja R(94950 58367), Dr. NADHEERA K M\nVolunteers 2025-26\nOperations Team: Lizona Loy Parayil, Muhammed Afsal N S, Anas\nTech Team: Gauri Smiju, Arya S\nCommunity Development Team: Anjima A J Kumar, Devanandhana S, Akshay S\nDesign Team: Anaswara G, Sanjana S Nair, Sooryaputhri D B, Ponnuru Sharoon\nMarketing Team: Joakim Manoj, Abhijith A S\nContent Team: Ajmal Mohammed Azim, Yasna Thasneem M, Aishwarya Shreyas\nInstagram Quiz\nAs part of the Indian Space Week 2024 celebrations, IEDC GECBH organized the Instagram Quiz Challenge from August 12th to 16th, 2024. Daily space-themed questions covering astronomy, space science, and ISRO missions were posted at 8:00 PM on Instagram. Supported by Kerala Startup Mission and LAUNCH, the event had a prize pool of ₹500 and saw enthusiastic participation from students.\nTechnical Talk on Plasma Analyser Package for Aditya (PAPA)\nOn September 12, 2024, IEDC hosted a technical talk by Dr. R. Satheesh Thampi, Head of the Planetary Science Branch at VSSC. Held at Zoom IT, the session focused on the Aditya-L1 mission and the role of the PAPA instrument in analyzing solar plasma. Dr. Thampi discussed technical challenges, mitigation strategies, and the significance of solar studies, concluding with an engaging Q&A session.\nIEDC Summit 2024\nThe IEDC Summit 2024, held at NIT Calicut, brought together innovators and entrepreneurs from across Kerala. The summit featured keynote speeches, technical workshops, startup expos, and live pitching sessions. GEC Barton Hill had an active presence, with multiple students participating in various events.\nInnovative Digests\nIEDC GECBH launched 'Innovative Digests', a weekly series of curated news articles on emerging technologies, groundbreaking projects, and innovation trends. This initiative aimed to keep students informed and inspired, fostering a culture of continuous learning and curiosity about the latest advancements in science and technology.\nIndustry-Academia Collaboration – Talk Session\nIn 2024, IEDC organized an Industry-Academia Collaboration session with professionals from Nexforce and Infosys. The event bridged the gap between academic learning and industry needs, highlighting applications of AI, cloud computing, and IoT. Students and faculty gained insights into curriculum improvements, project development, and internship opportunities.\nWomen in Startup – SPAx Event 2024\nOrganized in collaboration with IEEE WIE AG GEC Barton Hill and IEEE Kerala Section, this flagship event promoted women in entrepreneurship. It included panel discussions, mentorship circles, pitch sessions, and leadership workshops tailored for aspiring women entrepreneurs, creating a supportive and inclusive startup ecosystem.\nLogo Design Competition 2025\nIEDC launched a creative Logo Design Competition in 2025, inviting students, alumni, and creatives to design a logo reflecting innovation and entrepreneurship. Entries were judged based on creativity, originality, and relevance, strengthening IEDC’s visual identity and encouraging community involvement.\nAIntroduction to Project Management in Tech\nOn February 5, 2025, IEDC and the Institution’s Innovation Council hosted a webinar by Mr. Dinesh Venkateswaran, Manager at EY Ireland. The session introduced participants to project management methodologies, tools, and industry best practices, helping bridge the gap between classroom learning and real-world application.\nInnovative Digests IEDC GECBH\nlaunched its Innovative Digests, a weekly series featuring curated news articles on groundbreaking projects, emerging technologies, and innovation trends. These digests aim to spread awareness, foster curiosity, and highlight advancements in science and technology, making innovation more accessible to the campus community. Through regular updates, the initiative encourages students to stay informed and inspired by real-world developments in the innovation ecosystem.\nIntroduction to Project Management in Tech\nThe webinar titled “Introduction to Project Management in Tech” was scheduled on February 5, 2025, aiming to provide participants with a foundational understanding of project management within the tech industry. The session featured Mr. Dinesh Venkateswaran, Manager at EY Ireland, as the speaker. Organized by the Institution’s Innovation Council in collaboration with IEDC GECBH, the session promised valuable insights from industry practice, focusing on the key roles, methodologies, and tools used in modern tech project management.\nBeginner’s League\nThe Beginner’s League is a 1-week online tech competition conducted by IEDC GEC Barton Hill (GECBH) in collaboration with IEDC LBS. Designed to be inclusive and fun, the event welcomes students from nine colleges to explore, learn, and compete in a friendly innovation-driven environment\n“Unmute” – Group Discussion Event\nAs part of leadership and innovation skill-building, IEDC facilitated “Unmute,” a group discussion event designed to enhance students’ communication, critical thinking, and problem-solving abilities. This platform encouraged students to express their views on innovation-driven themes, thereby nurturing confidence and collaboration. In summary, the year 2024–25 has been a period of meaningful progress and renewed purpose for IEDC GEC Barton Hill. With continued enthusiasm and collective effort, we look forward to scaling greater heights in innovation and entrepreneurship in the years ahead. Thank you.\nSmart India Hackathon (SIH) 2025:\nThe Innovation and Entrepreneurship Development Cell (IEDC) of GEC Barton Hill actively participated in Smart India Hackathon (SIH) 2025, one of India’s largest nationwide innovation challenges aimed at fostering creative solutions to real-world problems. The top- performing teams from the internal hackathon were selected to represent GEC Barton Hill in the Smart India Hackathon 2025 National Round. These teams received guidance, mentoring, and technical support from IEDC to refine their solutions and enhance their presentations\nIEDC Ideathon 2025 (College Level Pitchfest Series)\nIEDC Ideathon 2025 was organized to identify and promote innovative ideas in Electronics and Technology, serving as the college-level qualifier for the Statewide Pitchfest Series. Over 25 teams registered, and 5 shortlisted teams presented their ideas in an online pitching session. Top teams were selected to represent GEC Barton Hill at the State-Level Pitchfest and will receive mentorship support. The event effectively encouraged entrepreneurship and creativity among students, contributing to the Kerala Startup Mission’s goal of fostering innovation-driven startups."
  },
