  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/wifi.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "The Campus Wi-Fi\nThe college is a member of the National Knowledge Network (NKN) which is a high-speed network backbone interconnecting major educational and research institutions. NKN is maintained by National Informatics Network (NIC), Government of India. NKN provides a bandwidth of 1000 Mbps (1 Gbps) to the college. In addition, a 16 Mbps internet connection has been taken from BSNL. This connection is being maintained as a backup to be used in the rare event of an NKN connection failure.\nThe network traffic between the college intranet and the internet is monitored and controlled through a firewall. Cisco routers and switches are used at the backbone level of the intranet. A fiber-optic backbone has been laid to provide connectivity to various parts of the campus.\nThe campus is completely connected by WiFi. A Ruckus ZoneDirector 3000 WiFi controller kept in the server room manages the WiFi access. Access points have been deployed throughout the campus so that online facilities like e-journals (which are provided on IP-based access) can be accessed from anywhere within the college premises.\nWi-Fi access will be available for all staff, students, guests\nThe various departments/sections covered under Wi-Fi are:\n- Main Administrative building (All 7 floors including Library)\n- Mechanical Lab Block (including ADAM, CADD Lab, PG Labs)\n- New Drawing Hall & proposed CADD Room (Civil) over the Mech Lab Block\n- EC/IT Block (All floors including the new IT Labs at 2nd floor)\n- Mechanical Workshop\n- New Computer Lab/CADD Lab (EEE dept)\n- Old Server Room and Labs (IT Department)\n- Proposed English Language Lab (Near old office)\n- MTech Class Rooms (old office)\n- PTA Building (All floors)\n- Mini Auditorium\n- Ladies Hostel (Lobby)\n- All main outdoor areas in campus including Canteen, NSS Room etc.\nAs part of a Cyber Agreement entered between our institution and Govt. of India, to make available high-speed internet connectivity (1000 Mbps, NKN connection) in campus, it is necessary that all user activities will have to be monitored, logged and recorded.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nA student desirous of getting a Wi-Fi/Internet account will have to register for the same. Copy of the Registration Form is available in the college website and at Students’ Facilitation Centre.\nThe filled-up Registration Form, duly recommended by the Staff Advisor, should be submitted at the IT Lab, EC/IT Block, on any working day.\nOn accepting an application, the student will receive an email from itsupport@gecbh.ac.in requesting him/her to forward his/her username and password (for WI-Fi / Internet access), by return mail.\nOn receipt of username/password as above, the student will be intimated about activation of his/her Wi-Fi/ Internet account through his/her registered e-mail ID.\nA staff desirous of getting a Wi-Fi / Internet account shall send a request by mail from his/her e-mail ID (as given in the Information Bulletin)with particulars of his/her Name, Designation and Department, addressed to itsupport@gecbh.ac.in. Any change in one’s mail-ID from that listed in Information Bulletin shall be intimated in advance before sending the request.\nHe/she will then receive a mail from itsupport@gecbh.ac.in requesting to forward his/her username and password (for WI-Fi / Internet access), by return mail. Thereafter, the concerned staff will be intimated about activation of his/her Wi-Fi/ Internet account through mail.\nThe details on how to activate a Wi-Fi / Internet account on receipt of a username and password will also be intimated through mail.\nWi-Fi will be available on Laptops, Desktops and in all devices (other than running Android application). Wi-Fi will be active in campus from 8.00 AM to 6.00 PM on all days.\nIn Ladies Hostel (lobby area), Wi-Fi will be active only from 6.00 AM to 8.00 AM and from 4.00 PM to 10 PM. All social media and networking sites are currently blocked. However, such sites will be open for limited usage from 12 Noon to 1.00 PM and from 4.00 PM to 5.00 PM on all days.\nIn the light of introduction of a user account system for Wi-Fi / Internet connectivity in campus, it is essential that all students/staff obtaining a WiFi / Internet account, leaving the institution, should report the IT department (Server Room) to surrender their account."
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/language-lab.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "A well equipped and well maintained language lab is maintained in the college to hone the soft skills of students. The students are trained in developing their communication skills through this system. The lab is equipped with server and clients with Express Pro Digital Lab software installed in it.\nCourse objective\nThe language lab lessons engaged in this college facilitates classroom engagement and interaction via computer based exercises and activities to maximise language immersion. It focuses on acquiring and developing the four main language skills of a student, namely; listening, speaking, reading and writing along with adequate grammar and vocabulary building exercises as well.\nCourse Contents\nThe components of the course currently being practiced under the language lab involves\n- 1. Audio – visual monologues with exercises.\n- 2. Pronunciation of words focusing on stress and intonation.\n- 3. Vocabulary building involving word meaning and usage in a sentence.\n- 4. Grammar exercises in MCQ model.\n- 5. Learning through conversation by incorporating a role play in the fill the blanks format.\n- 6. Exposure to various accents viz. American, British, Indian etc.\n- 7. Comprehension passage and further discussion of it in the format similar to group discussion.\n- 1. Understand the nuances of language through audio – visual experience and group activities.\n- 2. Speak with clarity and confidence thereby enhancing employability skills of the students.\n- 3. Perceive and comprehend the intricacies of grammar in an interactive fashion.\n- 4. Improve the fluency in spoken English and neutralize mother tongue influence.\n- 5. Familiarize students with the use of English in everyday situations and contexts.\n- 6. Furthermore, theoretical lessons on frequently used idioms and phrases, question tags, tenses and classroom conversations on specific topics have also been completed until now."
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/tbi.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "Technology Business Incubator\nTechnology Business Incubator - Government Engineering College Barton Hill (TBI-GECBH) was established in the year 2014 to provide a platform for budding entrepreneurs who wish to launch themselves into the world of technology-based business startups. The TBI-GECBH provides a platform for developing innovative and promising ideas into products or services business with the help of technology. This centre is designed to provide the support to business ventures of the students of GECBH as well as others.\nOBJECTIVES OF TBI-GECBH\n- To provide the infrastructure and environment required for starting technology business ventures through research, design, development, and training activities.\n- To encourage student entrepreneurship.\n- To accelerate the upgradation of research and development facilities available in the college to make them capable of carrying out leading edge research in technology.\n- To create employment / revenue generation / export possibilities through development of new technology-oriented products and services.\nFacilities and Support at TBI–GECBH\nGovernment Engineering College Barton Hill has established a dedicated space for its\nTechnology Business Incubator (TBI), comprising five fully furnished cubicles and a\ncommon area. This facility will be further expanded as additional space becomes\navailable on campus. TBI–GECBH provides each incubatee with a furnished cabin\nequipped with essential amenities such as electricity, internet connectivity, tables, and\nchairs. College facilities, including laboratories, workshops, and the library (for\nreference), can also be accessed with prior permission from the Principal on the\nrecommendation of the TBI Coordinator. Usage charges for these facilities will be\napplicable as determined by the respective departments. \nIncubatees may consult faculty members and technical staff for guidance and support in\ntechnical and business development activities, subject to their availability and\nwillingness. TBI–GECBH extends assistance to incubatees in availing various\ngovernment and private sector support schemes and helps them connect with consultants\nin finance, accounting, marketing, technology, quality, legal, human resources, and\ntraining. The incubator regularly organizes workshops, training programs, and seminars\non entrepreneurship and innovation based on demand. In addition, it provides procedural\nguidance for applying for patents, trademarks, and design registrations. Incubatees are\nalso encouraged to apply for relevant MSME schemes through which they may receive\nfinancial assistance via TBI–GECBH.\n| No | NAME OF THE VENTURE | MAIN PROMOTER NAME | EMAIL ID / WEBSITE | \n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ZELBYTES PVT LTD | Gourish G Pillai | Mail ID: [connect@nexforz.com](mailto:connect@nexforz.com)Website: [www.nexforz.com](https://www.nexforz.com) | \n| 2 | HESPER TECH PVT LTD | Viswanath S and Vimal George | Mail ID: [hespertechpvtltd@gmail.com](mailto:hespertechpvtltd@gmail.com)Website: [https://hespertech.in/](https://hespertech.in/) | \n| 3 | NexForz Ressync Private Limited | Gourish G Pillai | Mail ID: [info@zelbytes.com](mailto:info@zelbytes.com)Website: [www.nexforz.com](https://www.nexforz.com) | \n| 4 | REDFOX ROBOTICS | Vimun A and Jinso Raj | Mail ID: [startup.mail.robotics@gmail.com](mailto:startup.mail.robotics@gmail.com)Website: [www.redfoxrobotics.in](https://www.redfoxrobotics.in) | \nZELBYTES PVT LTD\nSmart Technology for Sustainable Farming\nAt Zelbytes Pvt. Ltd., we believe the future of farming lies in the intelligent\napplication of technology. We are an AgriTech company dedicated to revolutionizing\ntraditional agriculture by empowering farmers with AI-powered analytics and IoT-\nbased monitoring systems. Our core mission is to make farming more efficient,\nsustainable, and profitable by bridging the gap between established practices and\ncutting-edge innovation.\nOur flagship offering is a comprehensive smart farming solution designed for\nControlled Environment Agriculture, highlighted by our \"Autonomous Polyhouse\nwith a signature x,y,z coverable camera monitoring system,\" a project developed\nwith a prestigious grant from the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR). With\nsupport from national bodies and a key partnership with the Kerala Agricultural\nUniversity, we are developing indigenous technologies to solve real-world\nagricultural challenges.\nMoU with GECBH TBI\nAs a proud incubatee at the TBI-GECBH, Zelbytes leverages the vibrant engineering ecosystem of the Government Engineering College Barton Hill to accelerate its research and development. This strategic incubation provides us with invaluable access to advanced lab facilities, faculty expertise, and a pool of bright student talent. Our collaboration with TBI-GECBH is central to our goal of building a robust, indigenous AgriTech platform and fostering a culture of practical innovation among the next generation of engineers.\nHESPER TECH PVT LTD\nTransforming Ideas Into Impact\nAt Hesper Tech Pvt. Ltd. , we believe innovation means creating what truly works. We specialize in turning concepts into real, functional solutions — from product design and prototype development to reverse engineering, IoT-based system development, and specialized construction. Backed by a strong in-house R&D team, we combine practical engineering experience with smart design thinking and connected technology to deliver reliable, cost-effective innovations. We don’t just build products — we build intelligent solutions that make a lasting difference.\nOn April 23, 2025, Hesper Tech Pvt. Ltd. signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with the Technology Business Incubator (TBI), Government Engineering College Barton Hill. This collaboration was established to build a strong student team and provide them with hands-on exposure to real-world engineering challenges. The MoU serves as a bridge between academic learning and practical innovation — fostering collaboration, creativity, and product development experience for aspiring engineers.\nNexForz Ressync Private Limited\nEnd-to-End Innovation for Industry and Society\nAt Nexforz, we are a dynamic, technology-driven enterprise specializing in end-to-end product development across the IT, Electronics, and Mechanical domains. Our core mission is to design and deploy innovative technological solutions that address critical industry and societal challenges. We turn ambitious ideas into reality through our flagship R&D projects, including Rolit, a fully automated IoT bicycle rental system addressing urban mobility, and Recompute, an IaaS platform recognized by Startup India that democratizes access to AI computing.\nMoU with GECBH TBI\nStrategically located within the Government Engineering College, Barton Hill, our incubation at the TBI-GECBH is key to our goal of bridging the gap between theoretical research and real-world application. This partnership is central to our commitment to fostering a new generation of skilled technology professionals. We actively contribute to the ecosystem by conducting high-impact On-the- Job Training (OJT) in collaboration with Central and State Governments, and by delivering specialized training programs for senior professionals from leading technology corporations.\nEmpowering Communication Through Innovation\nAt RedfoxRobotics Pvt. Ltd., we believe that technology should speak for everyone. We are a Startup India–recognized and Kerala Startup Mission–supported startup driven by a mission to bridge the communication gap for the mute and speech-impaired community through advanced assistive technology.\nMou with GECBH TBI\nOur Team\nDR. BIJULAL D.\nPrincipal\nGovernment Engineering College\nBarton Hill, Thiruvananthapuram\nJAYASREE P\nTBI-GECB In-Charge\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Information Technology\nGovernment Engineering College Barton Hill\nThiruvananthapuram"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/iiic.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "An Industry Institute Interaction Cell (IIIC) was formed under TEQIP – II for monitoring the activities envisaged in the project. Interaction with industry and other research organizations through consultancy and sponsored research projects will make the faculty always in touch with the state-of-the-art technology. Industries are heavily concentrating on innovations and R&D activities to equip them globally competitive with their products and services. In the industrial scenario of Kerala, especially the State owned Public sector organizations are finding it difficult to cope up with the technology advancements due to lack of necessary R&D activities. Proper interaction with these industries will open opportunities for collaborative projects for consultancies and R &D. The institution can encourage the faculty, technicians and students to interact with industries in all possible ways with the spirit of delivering mutual benefit.\nThe institute signed MoU with more than five industries for collaborative activities for mutual benefits. Students, staffs, and faculty were able to visit and get hands on training in different specialised areas. MoU with different industries has increased tremendously the number of industrial student projects.It also helped in conducting value added education programs for students. The institute is presently offering a course “Advanced Diploma in Automotive Mechatronics (ADAM), in collaboration with Mercedes-Benz. The ADAM centre now offers BOSCH joint certification program. Revenue is also generated by these programs.\nStudents, Staffs and faculty were given different industrial training programs in their respective areas of interest so as to acquire new knowledge in the field as well as to make awareness of the advanced techniques and methods that are in use in the industry. Workshops were also conducted by experts from industry to get hands on training in new software’s the practice. Visit to premier institutes were also made to know more about the lab facilities and the work culture.\nStudents were sent for internship programs in various industries to get training in their respective areas of specialisation and to realise the difference between theory and practice. Internship also helped them to understand sustainability practices followed at industries. They were able to know more about the manufacturing process and different procedures followed.\nInternational and National level conferences and Colloquium were organized for the benefit of students and faculty. They were able to showcase their technical contributions/findings. These events surely provide them a platform to discuss and interact with the pioneers in the subject. The discussions with the subject experts have definitely enhanced their ideas,concepts, and the wok content.\nCoordinator\nDr. Rakesh P, Professor, ME\nMembers\nDr. Haripriya A.P., HoD, IT \nSmt. Frieda Mohan, Asst. Prof. in EEE"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/vedio-conference.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "Video Conferencing\nThe video conferencing facility was officially inaugurated by Sri. K. Muraleedharan, MLA on 27th March 2015.This room is equipped with LifeSize VC module, LED TV sets, LCD projectors, Audio Mixers, Microphones and Speakers.\nThe Video Conferencing room facilitate Direct Video conferencing with 3 centers at a time with seating capacity 72 and IP based dedicated VC units."
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.gecbh.ac.in/qeee.php",
    "title": "BartonHill",
    "content": "The Committee for Reforms in Technical Education under the chairmanship of Professor Ashok Jhunjhunwala, IIT Madras, has been constituted by the Ministry of Human Resources and Development (MHRD) with a mandate to bring about comprehensive reforms in technical education.\nThis committee has extensively studied the technical education system prevailing at present and identified problem areas in several colleges. One of the important concern areas is that the quality of technical education imparted in many colleges is not at par with the premier institutes. This in turn is because the colleges lack the pedagogical resources in the form of highly qualified teachers, well developed content and good lab infrastructure, that students in the premier institutes take for granted. To address this specific issue, the committee has come up with a program wherein real time and synchronous technology is used to allow for an integration of superior external scholastic inputs into current pedagogical practices.\nLive Class\nIIT faculty deliver live lecture classes to participating colleges. The schedule of lectures are sent to colleges priorly and notifications are provided before start of each session.\nCoursepack\nA supplement study material with lecture notes, live class videos, discussion forums, assessment and evaluation tools to serve as a reference material for QEEE courses.\nLive Labs\nTo provide virtual hands-on experience to students on experiments undertaken remotely. It creates an opportunity to work on a real lab experiment tailored to suit their syllabus and curriculum.\nBridge Programs\nTo bridge the gap between the initial skills of individuals and what they need to enter and succeed in postsecondary education and career-path employment."
  },
